  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...System bringing some rain showers tonight, \nfollowed by more showers and isolated thunderstorms Sunday \nafternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA couple rounds of 700 mb vort maxes will impact the forecast\narea beginning late tonight. First, a weak 700 mb vort max will\ndip over the St. John Valley overnight tonight. This may bring\nscattered rain showers into northern Aroostook county through\nthe night as some higher PWATs accompany the feature (lifting to\n0.8 to 0.9 inches), but the vorticity will weaken and exit the \narea early Sunday morning. Additionally, dry air will return as\nwell Sunday morning, with PWATs dipping back below 0.8 inches.\n\nA cold front will later cross the forecast area Sunday\nafternoon, bringing another round of showers and storms across\nthe entire area. Though forecast 0-6 km shear is quite high,\napproaching 50 kts from the NAMNEST, forecast CAPE is on the\nlower end, and there will not be any deep layer of moisture to\nwork with. That said, thunderstorms are possible but will likely\nbe ordinary in structure. Lines of training storms could develop\nalong the boundary itself as it crosses the area, and this would\nbe the best chance at any stronger organization for enhanced\nthreats, but even then, at most it appears maybe some gusty\nwinds with these storms based on the lack of extensive severe\nparameters being met at this time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...System brings round of rain showers mid-to-late\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over northern New England early week, as a low \npressure moves east ward through Canada. Global models are \ndisagreeing on the timing of the system and its associated \nfront, with the GFS showing rain on Thursday and the ECMWF \nshowing rain Wednesday afternoon. GLobal models have been \ntrending this system slower in the last few model runs, so \nanticipating rainfall late week. Expecting the highest rain\ntotals to be in the Downeast region. Confidence will begin to\nimprove once models get a better handle on the environment for\nnext week.",
